      "title": "Sentence 2 — a question embedded inside a comparison",
      "text": "**Original, B:** “Educational attainment records completed levels of education, whereas reading ability concerns what someone can actually do with a text.”\n\n**Plain-English paraphrase:** Qualifications describe completed education; reading skill describes practical ability with written material.\n\n**Structure:** Main clause + contrasting “whereas” clause, which contains an embedded what-clause.\n\n**Main clause:** “Educational attainment records completed levels of education.”\n\n**Dependent clauses:** “whereas reading ability concerns …” expresses contrast. “What someone can actually do with a text” functions as the complement of “concerns.”\n\n**Important grammar:** The embedded clause uses statement order: “what someone can …,” not “what can someone …”. “Completed” modifies “levels”; it is not the sentence's main verb.\n\n**Useful vocabulary:** educational attainment; reading ability; actually.\n\n**Why it sounds academic:** It separates a recorded credential from the ability that credential might be assumed to represent.\n\n**New IELTS-style example:** A driving certificate records formal qualification, whereas safe driving concerns what a person can actually do in changing conditions.",
